From 374736f2de1726f2303e97b70ee3b592b8292797 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: QueClr <51003352+QueClr@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 20:03:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] [conversion] lower the vector dialect without block merging
 (#1100)

The pipeline runs MLIR's `convert-vector-to-llvm` on every module. That
pass applies its first pattern sets with the greedy driver's default
configuration, and the default level of the driver's region
simplification is `Aggressive`.

What region simplification is for: after the patterns of an iteration
the driver keeps the control flow graph of each region up to date. At
the `Normal` level it removes unreachable blocks and dead operations
and block arguments. `Aggressive` adds block merging: blocks with the
same successors and the same operations become one block, which gets
an argument for each operand that differs, and arguments that every
predecessor passes the same value for are dropped.

Why MLIR's pass has it: it does not ask for it. The pass calls
`applyPatternsGreedily` without a configuration; `Aggressive` is the
driver's default, while MLIR's canonicalizer pass defaults to
`Normal`. The vector patterns rewrite operations and
create no blocks, so the merging does not serve the lowering: it
rewrites the blocks of every function of the module, also of one
without any vector. Nothing later in the pipeline needs it: the
canonicalizer runs after it at `Normal`, the inliner's canonicalizer
has region simplification disabled, and LLVM simplifies the control
flow graph itself.

Why this part is turned off: its cost has no bound. In a large
function with many similar arms the arguments of a merged block
multiply from one merge to the next. On one generated program (a state
machine in one large function) the pass ran 497 s and left blocks
with up to 234385 arguments, and LLVM needed another 7 minutes
for them: 924 s to the executable. With this change the pass runs
0.2 s and the build 9 s; the program's output is the same.

`reussir-convert-vector-to-llvm` applies the same pattern sets in the
same order, with that pass's default options and the same conversion
to the LLVM dialect. The one difference is the level `Normal`:
folding, constant CSE, and the removal of unreachable blocks and dead
operations and arguments stay. The pipeline and the JIT bridge use it.

What the merging gave, measured: it is a code size optimization that
was never chosen. Without it the lowered MLIR of 30 of the 33 units of
the benchmark suite, core and std has more blocks; their code is 0.7%
larger in the geometric mean (2.1% in total; from 23% smaller to 11%
larger). The code of 53 programs of a compiler that targets Reussir is
2.0% larger in the geometric mean (the largest 9.3%), with no
measurable effect on run time (geometric mean 1.000, 52 of 53 within
3%). The 13 SIMD tests give the same LLVM IR at `-O aggressive`; at
`-O none` two of them keep blocks that the merging joined.

Backport of upstream 317d326040310e072814231f0171392c5fddd788 (#1100) onto
the series; the content is upstream's, unchanged.
